**Latest Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) environment supporting multiple users, role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, revocation, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces authorization correctness, maintains delegation and permission consistency, and ensures safety and liveness under concurrency. Users have clearance levels (High, Medium, Low), and objects have classification levels (High, Medium, Low). Requests are classified by the highest classification level of affected objects and must comply with user clearance. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject lower-clearance user requests. Delegation and revocation of roles are governed by clearance and administrative rules. The system includes an Emergency Module to manage exceptional scenarios, which alters standard behaviors under specific triggers and constraints. Emergency mode is modeled as a fixed duration but may terminate early if both administrators who activated it jointly deactivate it. The system guarantees that Emergency mode always eventually ends and the system returns to Normal mode.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Emergency mode can only be triggered from Normal mode and requires exactly two distinct administrators present and acting concurrently to trigger entry; there is no requirement that two administrators must always exist in every session.
- Emergency mode is time-bounded (abstracted as a fixed duration), but may terminate early if both participating administrators jointly deactivate it; the system must always eventually return to Normal mode (liveness).
- FIFO ordering for request processing can be bypassed only during Emergency mode, and only for high-classification requests.
- Mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended only during Emergency mode, and only if two distinct emAdmins are present; this suspension must be tested as a specific scenario.
- Emergency privileges are granted only to the two distinct administrators who jointly triggered Emergency mode, only during Emergency mode, and must be revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends.
- Roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately at the Emergency-to-Normal transition. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode are not automatically revoked, but may still expire or be revoked by an administrator during Emergency mode.
- Only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger are required to update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Trigger  
Exactly two distinct administrators, both present as emAdmins and acting concurrently while the system is in Normal mode, are required to jointly trigger entry into Emergency mode. Emergency mode entry must be prevented if fewer than two administrators are present, if the same administrator is counted twice, or if the two do not act concurrently. Negative edge cases—such as attempted entry by only one administrator, or by the same administrator counted twice—must be explicitly tested and shown to be disallowed.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a limited (bounded) time, abstracted as a fixed duration, but may terminate early if both participating emAdmins jointly deactivate Emergency. The system must always eventually terminate Emergency mode and return to Normal mode (liveness guarantee).

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
R3.1. During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize and process multiple high-classification requests simultaneously.  
R3.2. During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold roles that are normally mutually exclusive, but only when two distinct emAdmins are present. This scenario must be specifically tested.  
R3.3. During Emergency mode, only the two distinct administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger temporarily hold emergency privileges.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1. After Emergency mode ends, emergency privileges must be revoked immediately from only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger.  
R4.2. After Emergency mode ends, any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately. Delegated roles from before Emergency mode remain subject to normal expiration or administrator revocation, including during Emergency mode.  
R4.3. After Emergency mode ends, only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger must update their credentials.

NEWLY DISCOVERED REQUIREMENT GAPS
==================================
- Negative test scenarios must be defined and verified: Emergency mode entry must be prevented in all cases where fewer than two distinct administrators are present, or where concurrent action cannot be established.
- All Emergency mode behaviors and suspensions (such as mutual exclusivity) are predicated on the presence of exactly two distinct emAdmins; these preconditions must be enforced and tested.

PROVISIONAL ASSUMPTIONS FOR VERIFICATION
========================================
- For purposes of verification, scenarios with fewer than two distinct administrators attempting to trigger Emergency mode are modeled and must result in Emergency mode not being entered.
- For concurrency modeling, "acting concurrently" is abstracted as a single transition step for both administrators.
```

